※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A cursor walks a list, and may take out what it is at.
※
※ `⎕iter(l)` is where a walk over the list in `l` begins.  It is given the place
※ and not the list, because a walk may take an element out: what is left has to
※ go back where the one holding the list will read it.  So what may be walked is
※ what may be written.
※
※ **`it⌖` is the element the walk is at**, read through the mark that asks what
※ is at a place, since that is what a cursor says.  **`⇧it` and `⇩it`** are the
※ cursor at the next element and at the one before; a walk that steps off either
※ end stops the program, there being nowhere for such a cursor to point and no
※ answer to give instead.
※
※ **`†it` takes out what the walk is at and answers the cursor at what
※ followed** -- which is the same cursor: what came after has moved down into
※ the place the element left, so the walk goes on from where it was.  Where what
※ went was the last element, the cursor is now one past the end, which is what a
※ walk that is over looks like.
※
※ **A cursor standing where a truth value is wanted asks whether the walk is
※ over**, which is the one question anything walking a list asks of it.  That is
※ what `unless` is for: `unless it:` runs the body until the walk is over, and
※ the removal loop needs no `¬` and no second name.
※
※ Compare: **C++**, whose `it = v.erase(it)` is this idiom exactly, and whose
※ iterators are invalidated by the removal unless the answer is taken -- here
※ there is nothing to invalidate, a cursor being where the list is and how far
※ along; **Rust**, whose `Vec::retain` is the loop written as a call and whose
※ iterators cannot remove at all; **Python**, where removing while walking is
※ the mistake every tutorial warns about; **Go**, which has neither.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let l: mut [u8] = [1u8, 2u8, 3u8, 4u8, 5u8, 6u8]
    let it: mut = ⎕iter(l)
    ※ every even element goes, and the walk goes on from where it was
    unless it:
        if ∣it⌖:
            it ← †it
        else:
            it ← ⇧it
    let sum: mut u8 = 0u8
    let many: mut u8 = 0u8
    foreach x := l:
        sum ← sum + x
        many ← many + 1u8
    if many ≠ 3u8:
        1u6
    elif sum ≠ 9u8:
        2u6
    else:
        moved()

※ Forwards and back, and what the walk is at either way.
@[impure]
fn moved() → u6:
    let l: mut [u16] = [10u16, 20u16, 30u16]
    let it: mut = ⎕iter(l)
    let first: u16 = it⌖
    it ← ⇧it
    let second: u16 = it⌖
    it ← ⇧it
    let third: u16 = it⌖
    it ← ⇩it
    let back: u16 = it⌖
    if first ≠ 10u16 ∨ second ≠ 20u16 ∨ third ≠ 30u16:
        3u6
    elif back ≠ 20u16:
        4u6
    ※ the walk is not over: it is at the second of three
    elif it:
        5u6
    else:
        ended()

※ A walk that is over, which is what taking out the last element leaves.
@[impure]
fn ended() → u6:
    let l: mut [u8] = [7u8]
    let it: mut = ⎕iter(l)
    let after: mut = †it
    if after:
        if #l ≠ 0:
            6u6
        else:
            ※ and a list with nothing in it has a walk that is over at once
            let empty: mut [u8] = []
            let none: mut = ⎕iter(empty)
            if none: 0u6 else: 7u6
    else:
        8u6
